    {
      "name": "PS-Simulink Converter",
      "referenceBlock": "nesl_utility/PS-Simulink Converter",
      "blockType": "SubSystem",
      "description": "",
      "category": "",
      "maskType": "PS-Simulink\nConverter",
      "maskDescription": "Converts the input Physical Signal to a Simulink output signal. \n\nFormat of Simulink output signal matches the format of Physical Signal. 'Vector format' provides an option for outputting vector Physical Signals as Simulink 1-D arrays. \n\nThe unit expression in 'Output signal unit' parameter must match or be commensurate with the unit of the Physical Signal and determines the conversion from the Physical Signal to the Simulink output signal. \n\n'Apply affine conversion' check box is only relevant for units with offset (such as temperature units).",
      "pathCategory": "",
      "metadataQuality": "high",
      "introducedIn": "2023a"
    },
    {
      "name": "Simulink-PS Converter",
      "referenceBlock": "nesl_utility/Simulink-PS Converter",
      "blockType": "SubSystem",
      "description": "",
      "category": "",
      "maskType": "Simulink-PS\nConverter",
      "maskDescription": "Converts the Simulink input signal to a Physical Signal.\n\nThe unit expression in 'Input signal unit' parameter is associated with the Simulink input signal and determines the unit assigned to the Physical Signal.\n\n'Apply affine conversion' check box is only relevant for units with offset (such as temperature units).\n\nIf the selected solver requires input derivatives, you can either provide them explicitly through additional signal ports, or turn on input filtering to calculate time derivatives. The first-order filter provides one derivative, while the second-order filter provides the first and second derivatives. For piecewise-constant signals, you can also explicitly set the input derivatives to zero.",
      "pathCategory": "",
      "metadataQuality": "high",
      "introducedIn": "2023a"
    },
